// Gimåpuppan: a high-floating CDC caddis pupa from the Gimån in Jämtland, an olive CDC body under a loose bubble of sand CDC.

id gimapuppan
name "Gimåpuppan"
aliases "Gimå Pupa"
type emerger
imitates caddis
naturals caddis-pupa
difficulty intermediate
species grayling trout
season june july august
origin region="Gimån, Jämtland, Sweden"
description "A body of olive CDC twisted into a rope, under a loose bubble of sand-coloured CDC, and a spiky thorax of mixed fur."
history """
    Published in Flugfiske i Norden in 2015 by its author, very likely David Stenström,
    whose initials are on the photos. Fishing the Gimån every summer when the caddis hatch
    is at its height, he found the Hjorthårspuppan floated too low to see, took a pattern
    called the Pite-puppan, simplified and changed it, and named the result after the river.
    It has been tried with success elsewhere in Sweden and in the Catskills. He does not claim
    the idea as new: many pupae use a bubble of CDC the same way.
    """
fishing """
    A caddis pupa floating high in the film, easy to see in poor light. Fish it dead drift,
    or pull it under and let it rise again like a hatching pupa. Treat it with CDC oil or a
    shake floatant, rinse and dry it with amadou after every fish, and switch to a deer-hair
    pupa after dark or in the thickest of the hatch, when there is no time to look after it.
    """
sources {
    "Flugfiske i Norden: Gimåpuppan" url="https://flugfiskeinorden.se/gimapuppan/"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://flugfiskeinorden.se/wp-content/uploads/2015/06/Puppa-SBS-101.jpg" page="https://flugfiskeinorden.se/gimapuppan/" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="The finished fly, side view in the vise"
}

// The author: a TMC 100BL dry-fly hook, or a pupa hook.
hook dry-standard size=14
thread black size="8/0"

materials {
    back cdc color="#c2a878" label="Sand CDC, two feathers"
    body cdc color=olive fibre=0.5 sweep=55 label="Olive CDC, two feathers"
    thorax dubbing color="#5a4a3a" shaggy=0.75 label="Fox, muskrat, opossum and seal, mixed"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.9 note="Wrap back a little way into the bend, then forward to about the point."
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    tie-in back note="Tips matched and pointing forward, tied in low on the feathers; pull them back until the tie-in nears the tips, then lock them."
    tie-in body note="The same way."
    wrap-thread to=0.68 note="To about 3 mm behind the eye."
    wind body to=0.68 turns=6 note="Twist the feathers into a rope first, with hackle pliers or the fingers."
    tie-off body
    // Read from the photo: the bubble stands about a third of a gape clear of the body.
    pull-over back to=0.68 bubble=0.3 note="Make it big first, then shrink it to size by pulling the ends, a dubbing needle inside it as a stop; lock it with hard turns."
    dub thorax to=0.92 note="Split the thread, put a little loose dubbing in, and twist 2 to 3 cm of it lightly, so it stays spiky."
    whip-finish note="A drop of thin lacquer."
}
